hilltop castle overlooking Chapultepec Forest, one of only two royal palaces in North America once inhabited by monarchs (Emperor Maximilian I & Empress Carlota), now home to the National Museum of History."},{name:"Museo Nacional de Antropología",description:"Premier world-class museum in Chapultepec showcasing profound pre-Columbian civilizations, featuring the monumental Aztec Sun Stone calendar."},{name:"Palacio de Bellas Artes",description:"Dramatic marble performance hall and museum in central Mexico City, featuring murals by Diego Rivera among other artists."},{name:"Museo Nacional de Arte (MUNAL)",description:"Architectural jewel on Calle de Tacuba housing five centuries of Mexican artistic heritage from the viceregal colonial era through 20th-century masters inside a regal neoclassical palace."},{name:"Palacio de Minería",description:"Neoclassical building in Mexico City's Centro Histórico, designed and built between 1797 and 1813 by Valencian sculptor and architect Manuel Tolsá to house the Royal School of Mines. Now part of UNAM's Faculty of Engineering, it houses the Tolsá Museum and temporary exhibitions."},{name:"Casa de los Azulejos (The House of Tiles)",description:"Exquisite 18th-century baroque count's residence whose exterior facade is covered on three sides in hand-painted blue-and-white Talavera ceramic tiles from Puebla."},{name:"Plaza de la Constitución (El Zócalo)",description:"Monumental central square and ceremonial bedrock of Mexico City, bordered by the National Palace and the buried foundations of ancient Tenochtitlan."},{name:"Xochimilco Canals (Embarcadero Nativitas)",description:"UNESCO World Heritage canal network in Xochimilco, navigated by colorful trajinera boats departing from Embarcadero Nuevo Nativitas, one of the main boat launches offering tours past floating gardens and chinampas."},{name:"Mercado de Artesanías La Ciudadela",description:"Long-running artisan market on Balderas in Mexico City's Centro, with about 350 vendors selling folk art and handicrafts from around Mexico, including alebrijes, Otomi textiles, jewelry and traditional clothing. Free entry."},{name:"Frida Kahlo Museum (The Blue House)",description:"Cobalt-blue house museum in Coyoacán, birthplace and lifelong home of Frida Kahlo. Preserves her personal belongings, artwork by Kahlo and Diego Rivera, folk art, and pre-Hispanic artifacts."}],activities:[{name:"Trajinera Boat Ride in Xochimilco",description:"Float along ancient Aztec agricultural canals on brightly decorated, flat-bottom boats."},{name:"Walking Tour of Polanco",description:"Stroll through tree-lined streets home to spectacular architecture and several synagogues."}],hotels:[{name:"JW Marriott Hotel Mexico City Polanco",description:"Sophisticated luxury hotel situated on Andrés Bello in the heart of Polanco overlooking Chapultepec Park, located directly inside the active Polanco Eruv and walking distance to multiple synagogues and premier kosher restaurants.",price:"$$$$",address:"Andrés Bello 29, Polanco, Polanco V Secc, Miguel Hidalgo, 11560 Ciudad de México, CDMX, Mexico",phone:"+52 55 5999 0000",website:"https://www.marriott.com/en-us/hotels/mexjw-jw-marriott-hotel-mexico-city-polanco/overview/",photo:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/jw-marriott-hotel-mexico-city-polanco-mexico-city.webp",photos:[],kosherFriendlyNotes:"In Polanco, which the community eruv covers (check its status before Shabbat). Gaucho Grill (kosher) is about 0.4 km away; Chabad Lubavitch (Calderón de la Barca 339) and Maguen David synagogue are each about 1.5 km, roughly a 20-minute walk. Rooms have a fridge."},{name:"InterContinental Presidente Mexico City",description:"Landmark 42-story luxury high-rise tower in Polanco on Campos Elíseos with sweeping skyline views of Chapultepec Park, situated fully within the Polanco Eruv and steps from Lincoln Park and top kosher dining.",price:"$$$$",address:"Campos Elíseos 218, Polanco, Polanco IV Secc, Miguel Hidalgo, 11560 Ciud